{"id": "clm:8e510794319e89a0e22d7200325125a216c70b73fe1029e4b2792cc5680da4b9", "type": "statement", "text": "Compressed on-disk collection size is not a direct estimate of WiredTiger cache demand: collection pages are uncompressed in the internal cache, while filesystem caching retains the on-disk representation. Indexes have their own representation and may retain prefix-compression benefits. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "definition", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-tuning-anti-patterns-how-tuning-memory-can-make-things-much-worse/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:ae5591aa99305f89dacb16514a009573ef8ad0c6ca8e69b409060794163c47cd"]}
{"id": "clm:4dece9242f7f2f2bd820c43daac8cbd74b3ef9fcf91135c034450200da54747f", "type": "statement", "text": "MongoDB advises avoiding increasing the WiredTiger internal cache size above its default value. MongoDB uses both the WiredTiger internal cache and the operating system's filesystem cache, which uses free memory not taken by the WiredTiger cache and is used by the operating system to reduce disk I/O. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage.md", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage.md", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-tuning-anti-patterns-how-tuning-memory-can-make-things-much-worse/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION", "evidence_ids": [], "remediation": {"schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1", "record": "remediation.json", "requires_revalidation": true, "claim_text_sha256": "f10c6eaa3b5f933cf0e873372a0544d3081f68f4e1ed5c3cdce849f5faba895d"}, "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "origin_evidence_ids": ["ev:398661093878790f027876966b13fc1e5426cf3a679afe75df92c07240ac8026", "ev:e8b682d681a7a4301d69fd66020d5ce6724b663a3133bba1671cf3a4caa40a4e"]}
{"id": "clm:dfccbe43f2ca1a0027ac2d48d36a84226675481ee4629cd9384a28c0a57cd9ad", "type": "statement", "text": "Connection capacity is a separate sizing constraint. Atlas tiers impose connection limits, and when an application creates a new MongoClient per request or process, each client can open its own pool up to maxPoolSize, multiplying total open connections. Each connection uses up to 1 megabyte of RAM. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/ ; https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/troubleshooting/connection-storms.md ; https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.coinbase.com/blog/scaling-connections-with-ruby-and-mongodb", "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/troubleshooting/connection-storms.md", "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION", "evidence_ids": [], "remediation": {"schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1", "record": "remediation.json", "requires_revalidation": true, "claim_text_sha256": "f57955b2f287c65f536b6421a683202a7b61cf7255f3863c1735ae1729b99f38"}, "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "origin_evidence_ids": ["ev:03bf3b751fa44b021dfcd9e17aee893318c4ac9a26582de7ee8bf2cda672ce96", "ev:48d54e8640a4c082aca7ee1100f3a713f29fc3ea6988dfa0e76947a31647f795", "ev:4991ef9cc526d3b49eef1fec6753a177e460cbf377cd562a611aff12c7402219"]}
{"id": "clm:dbf0204069e6ee08b96fca0366755b32e163d8d61e71ab841d3baf8e45c240c0", "type": "statement", "text": "Sizing only for steady-state application topology misses deployment and recovery load. Coinbase reported doubled connections during blue-green deployments; CircleCI reported scaling services during database degradation. Inference: include overlapping deployments and backlog recovery when assessing capacity headroom. (medium confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.coinbase.com/blog/scaling-connections-with-ruby-and-mongodb", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://discuss.circleci.com/t/postmortem-march-26-april-10-workflow-delay-incidents/30060"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:0f05393506504ec5c4a460dc05cd117fe4a250b84940ef9d5085ada8c63f14e4", "ev:7d7c9116fa7638c2ca89027f43924137643fe523a7b1b086edd86fc7be75d272", "ev:e1fc3f950ee7291f331429dcdfcca5ecb780b852344784394c31501cf3ba1e24"]}
{"id": "clm:567944df56760cba9d202d2f27ffd1eafc73610fe4c3ff4e29f8aa04d4d0852d", "type": "statement", "text": "Dropping an index merely because one node reports zero accesses can make a capacity reduction unsafe. MongoDB documents that $indexStats reports usage only for the node where the query runs (so run it on each node) and recommends hiding indexes to evaluate their impact before dropping them. Percona notes that the ops counter is reset when mongod restarts, so a zero count means unused only since the index was added or the server restarted. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/design-antipatterns/unnecessary-indexes/ ; https://www.percona.com/blog/identifying-unused-indexes-in-mongodb/", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/design-antipatterns/unnecessary-indexes/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.percona.com/blog/want-mongodb-performance-you-will-need-to-add-and-remove-indexes/", "https://www.percona.com/blog/identifying-unused-indexes-in-mongodb/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "NOT-SAMPLED", "evidence_ids": [], "remediation": {"claim_id": "clm:24a40f49c172cbad3ac0cde6abb2124289b0fa57eaa6e2aaf04083638e923352", "outcome": "CORRECTED", "at": "2026-11-02T09:00:49Z"}}
{"id": "clm:58858cdd8792609f6dc89b539abbbc6d84399f52b281cb92bd3e784f9a4b1506", "type": "statement", "text": "Connection capacity planning should include application process count, independent pools, and deployment overlap. Coinbase documented per-process pools and blue-green deployment connection storms; Atlas documentation separately identifies tier-dependent connection limits. Larger database tiers are therefore not the only possible response to excessive connection demand. (medium confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.coinbase.com/blog/scaling-connections-with-ruby-and-mongodb", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:03bf3b751fa44b021dfcd9e17aee893318c4ac9a26582de7ee8bf2cda672ce96", "ev:4991ef9cc526d3b49eef1fec6753a177e460cbf377cd562a611aff12c7402219", "ev:78b0e993a3dbdf664d3f252fffd30231c99ed118d7afb383fdd8b67a98789d9b"]}
{"id": "clm:9d94f1dc4d3b65374eee836b55e8d5eb3af7dd6ac522debeb6d53882effe7ae3", "type": "statement", "text": "Hardware upgrades do not repair every apparent capacity problem. In CircleCI’s 2019 incident, switching to local SSD storage did not resolve saturation; repeated index declarations, lock contention, and memory-management behavior also contributed. This is historical failure evidence, not a claim about current MongoDB internals. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://discuss.circleci.com/t/postmortem-march-26-april-10-workflow-delay-incidents/30060", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "problems", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:855c7141016376d5807014a78ee1c2b47280d90995a33ddc5e7508a8f52e0893", "ev:950120bf277e4ac54b12ffdb86831708791a3d18abc1b75c8777b96bb2030361"]}
{"id": "clm:e01f3b6f6c9a4e761fef936539291f3c58ac14f0955d4c9cb8a3e2bcd1175a25", "type": "statement", "text": "Self-managed container sizing must respect the container memory limit and leave room beyond the WiredTiger cache. MongoDB says that when mongod runs in a container without access to all system RAM, the cache size must be set to less than the RAM available in the container, by an amount that depends on the other processes in the container. This is a container-budgeting pitfall, not an Atlas configuration recipe. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "problems", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://github.com/Netcracker/qubership-mongodb-operator/blob/main/docs/public/troubleshooting.md"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION", "evidence_ids": [], "remediation": {"schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1", "record": "remediation.json", "requires_revalidation": true, "claim_text_sha256": "3beac86e26b00f115670a0031bb5915e1b1521d602bb4a81be94cdace62f7faf"}, "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "origin_evidence_ids": ["ev:6eb3122328572afe71d4254911540eb525d3f737abab45ba474620d7419e785b", "ev:94084ea7dfba8c66966a28cfebbb656c438ed564b25adfc01703d4ff67e7b89a"]}
{"id": "clm:8a97786da09b8d4b6d58952141fd557138912226dd615c8a19e9fab076016b56", "type": "statement", "text": "Sizing RAM to hold the entire database can overprovision workloads that repeatedly access only a subset of documents; conversely, sizing only for document bytes omits indexes. MongoDB and Percona base memory planning on the frequently accessed working set, including relevant indexes, rather than total stored data alone. (high confidence) Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/ ; https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-101-how-to-tune-your-mongodb-configuration-after-upgrading-to-more-memory/", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "comparisons", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.percona.com/blog/thinking-about-deploying-mongodb-read-this-first/", "https://www.percona.com/blog/mongodb-101-how-to-tune-your-mongodb-configuration-after-upgrading-to-more-memory/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION", "evidence_ids": [], "remediation": {"schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1", "record": "remediation.json", "requires_revalidation": true, "claim_text_sha256": "9939811af3d47bca7dd72de4f8ca5f3a0e52552b2f879c407e43c5ecd63a6e02"}, "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "origin_evidence_ids": ["ev:1c21922e5340b652a38473776da22703de86b3b29af77738f502248013dce718", "ev:730a41371ebeed549671327c71a17d6fb3377226efaddfedb5894d08109fe974", "ev:d9d6cc9ccd41c6da9fcdff83543bbf509d22f3d53ff282529d215c5820f850a0"]}
{"id": "clm:dc4f20a0d60ba83a2b186f7bafb121f3d475472fd7e3d48f10c9c51d20cd9653", "type": "statement", "text": "Ignoring bandwidth can leave a RAM-based tier choice inadequate. Atlas sizing guidance says to consider the maximum traffic borne by an individual node and the nodes that actually serve reads, rather than assuming cluster traffic is evenly distributed. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "comparisons", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:5116a6361481bac53ddd9b4ef852e408a76d6db15d96b7c7a2421cecdbd2df44"]}
{"id": "clm:c14a5e5e61915f35ede4c0bb18b4905004d903086906ef6c4e3fb1eddd3c355d", "type": "statement", "text": "Treating advertised Atlas RAM as available cache overstates capacity: the advertised memory is physical RAM per data-bearing node, and memory must also support operations and system services. Replica-set node RAM is not one pooled cache. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:faacafc7261bf217ad6a4a957c0a4a591616f4b9783dd3b07c705bb8bd8f54f6"]}
{"id": "clm:dcd3d70471b10ff47903248831a5f71c3efc33795cba07672f63b8730ceb5c48", "type": "statement", "text": "Adding indexes indiscriminately can manufacture a need for larger hardware: extra indexes increase storage, write-maintenance work, and cache pressure. Review redundant and unused indexes before treating every slowdown as insufficient cluster capacity. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.percona.com/blog/want-mongodb-performance-you-will-need-to-add-and-remove-indexes/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/design-antipatterns/unnecessary-indexes/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:802db4cb0dd96c1fc50e36cca265061c99e565d19f23fd60c06cf827959c26e5", "ev:ab4a9627e638b5fdc4bdf1f5c09cbaa9903109261eba5e17da1403e2d584916a", "ev:b6eac967f3554533ccefddcb596da638a4f4b9dc7b3d2fa77e1410518abf5940"]}
{"id": "clm:a0ca2261528a8e01695e8bdc5f55107be78cb99f474450894c8cd5d03b443dbc", "type": "statement", "text": "Allocating all node RAM to the database cache understates total memory needs. Filesystem caching, the operating system, client connections, sorts, and aggregation work also consume memory. (high confidence) Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/ ; https://docs.percona.com/percona-monitoring-and-management/3/advisors/checks/mongodb-high-wiredTiger-cache.html", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.percona.com/blog/thinking-about-deploying-mongodb-read-this-first/", "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/sizing-tier-selection/", "https://docs.percona.com/percona-monitoring-and-management/3/advisors/checks/mongodb-high-wiredTiger-cache.html"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION", "evidence_ids": [], "remediation": {"schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1", "record": "remediation.json", "requires_revalidation": true, "claim_text_sha256": "f438d0b07b6d7cb3e1865ccff0dec087b11b56e2c2db07b2ecbb04ea9b0c8127"}, "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "origin_evidence_ids": ["ev:66ce3fe46c9a10b90e2f301514c8d6e0e736bf482360ba3b2bf1358686ba368f", "ev:e854dd8109107458e8eba368081bef366506d4b5722dac3920441a7c3f89a6ca"]}
{"id": "clm:676dd5c8860f659eeecd4939a2992800eebab5c444b4cbbed8461fa25989213b", "type": "statement", "text": "A connection bottleneck need not mean useful database work requires more compute. In its June 18, 2020 engineering account, Coinbase found many connections mostly unused and reduced connection demand through multiplexing. MongoDB also documents memory overhead for connections. This historical case does not establish current driver defaults or a general savings factor. (medium confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.coinbase.com/blog/scaling-connections-with-ruby-and-mongodb", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Common Sizing Mistakes"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/faq/storage/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:ce1fd605ec941fb58ebc6810b2082775e3320ea1bbe327d35456a9c4346aa336", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:78b0e993a3dbdf664d3f252fffd30231c99ed118d7afb383fdd8b67a98789d9b", "ev:938a37ee957e1b90da4503e5bf537d92989aace46ba2989eb911af5e86ecd578", "ev:b0817e0745bf874db5f0bb7bd1eb3c09dbbd96110f8d7361400e6c0bdb720113"]}
